WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.083 --> 00:00:03.461
- Hey, Did you just skip that ad?

00:00:03.461 --> 00:00:05.463
The one that plays before this video?

00:00:05.463 --> 00:00:07.465
Well, thanks a lot.

00:00:07.465 --> 00:00:09.384
Now we don't get the ad money.

00:00:09.384 --> 00:00:12.303
It's like you ripped
the eight tenths of a cent

00:00:12.303 --> 00:00:14.305
right out of my child's mouth.

00:00:14.305 --> 00:00:16.766
- Welcome to the creator economy,

00:00:16.766 --> 00:00:19.853
a vast community of writers,
artists, personalities,

00:00:19.853 --> 00:00:23.690
and... pets? --who make money by
posting original content

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:27.402
on one of a dozen or so
major social media platforms.

00:00:27.402 --> 00:00:30.238
It's one of the fastest growing
industries in the world,

00:00:30.238 --> 00:00:31.656
with a recent study, predicting

00:00:31.656 --> 00:00:34.200
that 50 million people worldwide

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.703
will soon consider themselves creators.

00:00:36.703 --> 00:00:39.039
- But is it really a job,

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:42.375
or more of a small business,
or just a crap shoot?

00:00:42.375 --> 00:00:44.961
(bright music)

00:00:50.675 --> 00:00:54.512
There are several ways to make
money as a content creator,

00:00:54.512 --> 00:00:56.681
and the oldest is advertising.

00:00:56.681 --> 00:00:59.476
Most of the major
platforms offer some kind

00:00:59.476 --> 00:01:02.145
of ad revenue sharing with their creators.

00:01:02.145 --> 00:01:05.690
The amounts vary depending
on popularity and platform.

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:08.735
TikTok will pay as
little as 30 or 40 bucks

00:01:08.735 --> 00:01:10.278
for 1,000,000 views,

00:01:10.278 --> 00:01:13.156
while the same number on
YouTube can get you thousands.

00:01:13.156 --> 00:01:16.409
- This is still generally
not enough to pay the bills,

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:19.454
so many creators look
for brand sponsorships,

00:01:19.454 --> 00:01:21.664
when advertisers pay them directly

00:01:21.664 --> 00:01:24.084
for promoting products within the content.

00:01:24.084 --> 00:01:25.835
You can also sell your own merch,

00:01:25.835 --> 00:01:28.588
or even start your own product
line like fashion, cosmetics,

00:01:28.588 --> 00:01:30.757
or exercise equipment.

00:01:30.757 --> 00:01:32.926
Of course, all these options only exist

00:01:32.926 --> 00:01:37.764
for creators that already have
a large, dedicated fan base.

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:40.100
- A recent trend offers
creators more direct ways

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:41.726
to monetize.

00:01:41.726 --> 00:01:45.230
Sites like Patreon, Substack,
and OnlyFans allow artists,

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:47.607
writers, and personalities

00:01:47.607 --> 00:01:51.027
to sell access to their work
directly to their audience.

00:01:51.027 --> 00:01:54.405
Twitch lets you financially
support your favorite streamers,

00:01:54.405 --> 00:01:57.408
and even Twitter now has a tipping option.

00:01:57.408 --> 00:01:58.576
- Wow!

00:01:58.576 --> 00:02:00.411
With all these money making avenues,

00:02:00.411 --> 00:02:01.996
it seems like being a creator

00:02:01.996 --> 00:02:04.833
might not be such a terrible
financial decision after all.

00:02:04.833 --> 00:02:07.377
- Well, we won't know until we-

00:02:07.377 --> 00:02:09.087
- [Both] Run the numbers.

00:02:09.087 --> 00:02:12.006
(triumphant music)

00:02:14.092 --> 00:02:16.678
- Two years ago, Kyle
started a YouTube channel

00:02:16.678 --> 00:02:19.222
to showcase his ukulele skills.

00:02:19.222 --> 00:02:21.432
After a lot of dedication and hard work,

00:02:21.432 --> 00:02:25.061
he now manages to put out
a quality video every week,

00:02:25.061 --> 00:02:28.565
and gets a respectable
80,000 views on average.

00:02:28.565 --> 00:02:33.194
That puts him squarely in the
top 5% of YouTubers on earth.

00:02:33.194 --> 00:02:34.696
Nice work, Kyle.

00:02:34.696 --> 00:02:35.613
- For his success,

00:02:35.613 --> 00:02:39.993
YouTube pays him a weekly
ad share revenue of $200.

00:02:39.993 --> 00:02:41.619
- Okay, not great, but-

00:02:41.619 --> 00:02:44.747
- Oh wait, forgot to
take out YouTube's cut.

00:02:44.747 --> 00:02:45.874
- Ouch.

00:02:45.874 --> 00:02:46.708
That's okay,

00:02:46.708 --> 00:02:49.794
because Kyle is finally getting
some branded sponsorships,

00:02:49.794 --> 00:02:51.045
about one a month.

00:02:51.045 --> 00:02:52.630
He's also selling a bit of merch

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:54.591
and has a dozen Patreon supporters

00:02:54.591 --> 00:02:59.012
for a grand monthly total of $2,343.

00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:01.931
Not bad for a side hustle.

00:03:01.931 --> 00:03:03.224
Wait, what's that, Kyle?

00:03:03.224 --> 00:03:04.934
This isn't your side hustle?

00:03:04.934 --> 00:03:08.688
You quit your job for this?

00:03:08.688 --> 00:03:10.648
- Most serious creators will tell you

00:03:10.648 --> 00:03:12.817
this is no part-time gig,

00:03:12.817 --> 00:03:15.695
between the writing, shooting,
editing, replying to fans,

00:03:15.695 --> 00:03:19.449
making graphics, looking for
sponsors, studying analytics,

00:03:19.449 --> 00:03:21.826
designing merch, and running a Patreon,

00:03:21.826 --> 00:03:23.036
(sighs)

00:03:23.036 --> 00:03:24.454
managing a successful channel

00:03:24.454 --> 00:03:28.249
can overwhelm a team of
people, let alone one.

00:03:28.249 --> 00:03:30.126
When you factor in equipment costs

00:03:30.126 --> 00:03:33.463
and the two years he spent
building his audience,

00:03:33.463 --> 00:03:37.926
Kyle is working far below
minimum wage on this dream job.

00:03:37.926 --> 00:03:40.970
- Meanwhile, his job security and benefits

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:42.972
are practically nill.

00:03:42.972 --> 00:03:44.682
He's busting his hump to create content

00:03:44.682 --> 00:03:48.770
for a giant tech company that
gives him no health insurance,

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:51.606
no retirement, and at any
time could make changes

00:03:51.606 --> 00:03:53.566
to their algorithm or revenue plan

00:03:53.566 --> 00:03:56.236
that totally disrupts
his business strategy.

00:03:56.236 --> 00:03:58.446
- Kyle's predicament is sadly common

00:03:58.446 --> 00:04:00.740
for successful content creators.

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:04.118
Sure, the handful of top
channels can pull in millions,

00:04:04.118 --> 00:04:08.206
but earnings drop steeply
as you go down the ladder.

00:04:08.206 --> 00:04:11.542
One study estimated that
you can be in the top 3%

00:04:11.542 --> 00:04:13.795
of YouTubers and make less

00:04:13.795 --> 00:04:16.839
than one third the median US income.

00:04:16.839 --> 00:04:19.842
In order to make the us
median income on Twitch,

00:04:19.842 --> 00:04:24.514
you have to be in the top
0.015% of active streamers,

00:04:24.514 --> 00:04:26.849
for the median income.

00:04:26.849 --> 00:04:29.978
- And those who are
already wealthy and famous

00:04:29.978 --> 00:04:31.938
enjoy a massive head start.

00:04:31.938 --> 00:04:36.025
Having a glamorous life as a
backdrop gives you a big edge

00:04:36.025 --> 00:04:37.652
in the battle for eyeballs.

00:04:37.652 --> 00:04:39.237
And who else would have the time

00:04:39.237 --> 00:04:42.448
to spend years building
an audience for no pay?

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:45.034
If you look at the highest
earning Instagram accounts,

00:04:45.034 --> 00:04:46.953
you'll notice that they all got famous

00:04:46.953 --> 00:04:49.289
via traditional media.

00:04:49.289 --> 00:04:51.666
I wouldn't exactly call them successes

00:04:51.666 --> 00:04:53.501
of the creator economy.

00:04:53.501 --> 00:04:56.129
- Even for those who
manage to eke out a living,

00:04:56.129 --> 00:04:58.923
burnout is a constant hazard.

00:04:58.923 --> 00:05:02.593
Unlike a musician or filmmaker
that can release a new album

00:05:02.593 --> 00:05:04.220
or film every few years,

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:07.974
creators must be constantly
posting new content

00:05:07.974 --> 00:05:10.143
just to not fall behind.

00:05:10.143 --> 00:05:13.730
Audiences and algorithms
punish time off severely,

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:17.900
and with all the harassment,
toxicity, and judgment

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:19.110
on social media,

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:21.612
the spotlight can take an emotional toll

00:05:21.612 --> 00:05:24.991
on even the most successful
online celebrities.

00:05:24.991 --> 00:05:29.287
- And yet, millions of people
still aspire to be a creator.

00:05:29.287 --> 00:05:34.292
29% of Gen Zers said they
wanted to be a YouTube star.

00:05:35.043 --> 00:05:36.419
If it's your dream,

00:05:36.419 --> 00:05:39.047
probably nothing we've
said has changed your mind.

00:05:39.047 --> 00:05:39.881
Why?

00:05:39.881 --> 00:05:42.342
Because there's also a
non-financial benefit

00:05:42.342 --> 00:05:43.885
that creators are pursuing.

00:05:43.885 --> 00:05:47.180
- Call it fame, attention, or clout.

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.807
Humans have always had a keen awareness

00:05:49.807 --> 00:05:52.769
of where they rank in
the social hierarchy,

00:05:52.769 --> 00:05:56.689
and social media has
turbocharged that need.

00:05:56.689 --> 00:05:59.609
As we live more and more
of our lives online,

00:05:59.609 --> 00:06:02.904
our perception of self
worth has become linked

00:06:02.904 --> 00:06:04.405
to likes and follows.

00:06:04.405 --> 00:06:07.950
A handful of tech companies
are now the arbiters

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:10.453
of how status is allocated,

00:06:10.453 --> 00:06:14.290
and the more we battle for
it, the more money they make.

00:06:14.290 --> 00:06:15.291
- There's a lot of talk

00:06:15.291 --> 00:06:18.586
about how fast the
creator economy is growing.

00:06:18.586 --> 00:06:21.923
But if you look at where the
investment dollars are going,

00:06:21.923 --> 00:06:23.758
it's not to the creators themselves,

00:06:23.758 --> 00:06:28.304
but to companies that sell
tools and services to creators.

00:06:28.304 --> 00:06:31.766
Pietra helps influencers
launch product lines,

00:06:31.766 --> 00:06:35.019
Pearpop monetizes your
social media interactions,

00:06:35.019 --> 00:06:37.647
and NewNew lets you charge fans

00:06:37.647 --> 00:06:40.066
to vote on what you have for breakfast.

00:06:40.066 --> 00:06:41.192
In this way,

00:06:41.192 --> 00:06:44.487
the creator economy is less
like an artist marketplace

00:06:44.487 --> 00:06:46.197
and more like a gold rush.

00:06:46.197 --> 00:06:49.325
A very small few will strike it rich.

00:06:49.325 --> 00:06:51.619
The majority crawl home empty handed,

00:06:51.619 --> 00:06:53.496
but the smart money is on the people

00:06:53.496 --> 00:06:55.373
who sell the picks and shovels.

00:06:55.373 --> 00:06:57.375
- There are a lot of companies out there

00:06:57.375 --> 00:07:00.628
that want to make being
a creator seem easier

00:07:00.628 --> 00:07:02.839
and more profitable than it really is

00:07:02.839 --> 00:07:05.967
because they make money off the dreamers.

00:07:05.967 --> 00:07:08.511
- Julia and I have done
pretty well as creators,

00:07:08.511 --> 00:07:12.056
but we're part of a big team
that makes this show a success,

00:07:12.056 --> 00:07:14.100
and almost all of us have other jobs.

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:16.978
And we have the financial
and marketing support

00:07:16.978 --> 00:07:19.230
of a large organization behind us.

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:21.899
- I promise, we're not
here to crush your dreams.

00:07:21.899 --> 00:07:25.903
Being a successful artist or
entertainer requires talent,

00:07:25.903 --> 00:07:28.197
hard work, and a realistic assessment

00:07:28.197 --> 00:07:30.032
of the financial landscape.

00:07:30.032 --> 00:07:32.952
- So if you are one of
the 50 million creators

00:07:32.952 --> 00:07:36.289
across the globe, by all
means, follow your dreams.

00:07:36.289 --> 00:07:38.624
Just make sure they're your dreams

00:07:38.624 --> 00:07:42.128
and not ones that have been
sold to you by someone else.

00:07:42.128 --> 00:07:44.380
- [Both] And that's our two cents.

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:45.756
- [Julia] Thanks to our patrons

00:07:45.756 --> 00:07:48.509
for keeping Two Cents financially healthy.

00:07:48.509 --> 00:07:49.886
Click the link in the description

00:07:49.886 --> 00:07:52.263
to become a Two Cents patron.

00:07:54.098 --> 00:07:56.684
- If you've dipped your
toe in the creator economy,

00:07:56.684 --> 00:07:59.353
let us know how it's going
in the comments below.

00:07:59.353 --> 00:08:02.064
(cheerful music)

